    "done_when": "tests/test_direct_resume_needs_an_observed_death.py builds runs in a temporary crew home, then calls resume_plan for each: one whose supervisor recorded an exit (resume plan returned), one whose checked local pid is found dead (plan returned), one whose launcher_host is another host with no exit record (refused, the refusal naming unknown liveness), one whose local process is proven alive (refused as today). The unknown-liveness case fails at the base revision because a plan is returned (logged) and passes at the head. The negative control, in a scratch copy, restores the proven-alive-only refusal; the unknown-liveness case fails, with the mutation on the red log's first line and an EXIT= line after pytest's output. The gate is the new test plus every existing test file that calls resume_plan or reckon crew resume; both arms are recorded as one JSON object each under baseline_suite and after_suite (revision, command, exit_status, one log_path, failure_ids as a JSON list), each log ending in an EXIT= line, with the head arm adding no failing id. If closing this guard reddens existing fixtures that resumed an unobserved run, the manifest lists them with status blocked rather than weakening the guard. ruff check plus ruff format --check on the changed paths add no finding against the base. The tests neither read nor write the real ~/.config/reckon, and start no process they do not terminate.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Plan a-stalled-row-says-whether-the-worker-lives, followup f-asrswtwl-direct-resume-needs-observed-death: a hand-typed resume waits for an observed end, as the resume sweep now does. Read the followup in docs/plans/a-stalled-row-says-whether-the-worker-lives.html. resume_plan in reckon/crew/dispatch.py (near :7634) refuses only a run whose record_process_alive reading is True, so reckon crew resume --run on a run of unknown liveness (another host's launcher, no exit record) proceeds as though the worker were dead, which may duplicate a live worker. The sweep's guard in reckon/crew/resumption.py (merged at c4063efe) already requires an observed end: a pid this host checks, finding it dead, or a supervisor-recorded exit. Make resume_plan apply that same rule by reusing the sweep's helper, refusing a run of unknown liveness with its reason named. A run with a recorded session whose worker is proven dead resumes exactly as today.",
